---
/**
 * What leaves your Mac, and what does not.
 *
 * Written as an inventory rather than a policy, because for this product the
 * honest version is very short and a page of boilerplate would obscure it: the
 * app makes exactly one network request of its own, and the only personal data
 * anything here stores is a purchase email.
 *
 * Every claim below is checkable against something in the repository, and the
 * links say which — a privacy page whose assertions cannot be verified against
 * the code is the same class of claim as a marketing screenshot of a feature
 * that does not exist.
 */
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import { DOCS, REPO_URL, SITE_URL } from "../config";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

/**
 * `dataCollected: []` is not decoration. Schema.org has no way to say "none",
 * so the absence of a `dataCollected` entry is the only machine-readable way to
 * state it, and an omitted property would read as unanswered instead.
 */
const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: "Privacy",
  url: `${SITE_URL}/privacy`,
  isPartOf: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/#website` },
  publisher: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/#organization` },
};

/** What the app sends, in full. There is one entry, and that is the point. */
const LEAVES = [
  {
    what: "An update check",
    when: "Only after you turn updates on, then once a day.",
    body: "Sparkle fetches an appcast from GitHub. `SUSendProfileInfo` is false and `SUEnableAutomaticChecks` is present-and-false in the Info.plist rather than absent, because an absent key is what makes Sparkle ask on second launch — this way a fresh install has already answered, and the answer is no.",
  },
] as const;

/** What never does. Each of these is enforced somewhere nameable. */
const STAYS = [
  {
    what: "Your credentials",
    body: "They are in the Keychain, handed to a child process at spawn and never anywhere else. Nothing in the app and nothing any client can call returns one once it is set. The built-in server can say which secrets a profile has; it cannot say what they are.",
  },
  {
    what: "The activity log",
    body: "A ring buffer in memory, cleared when the app quits. Nothing writes it to disk, and nothing uploads it. Arguments are redacted before a row is recorded rather than filtered out afterwards.",
  },
  {
    what: "Everything the servers do",
    body: "A supervised server talks to whatever API it is for, using the credentials you gave that profile. Bastion is the process holding the credential, not a proxy for the traffic — it never sees or forwards a response body to anyone but the client that asked.",
  },
  {
    what: "Your licence key",
    body: "Verification is an Ed25519 signature check on your own Mac. There is no activation call, no licence server, and nothing that phones home to count installs.",
  },
] as const;
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy — Bastion"
  description="What leaves your Mac and what does not: one optional update check, no analytics, no telemetry, credentials in the Keychain and an activity log that never touches disk."
  schema={schema}
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="wrap py-20">
    <h1 class="m-0 font-display text-[clamp(28px,4.4vw,40px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.04em]">
      Privacy
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[16px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
      Bastion is a local daemon that holds every credential you own. The only defensible privacy
      posture for that is to send nothing, and the only way to make the claim worth anything is to
      say what would falsify it. Everything below is checkable in
      <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener" class="underline underline-offset-2">the source</a>.
    </p>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="m-0 font-display text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]">
        What the app sends
      </h2>
      <dl class="m-0 mt-6 flex flex-col gap-6">
        {
          LEAVES.map((item) => (
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-start gap-x-10 gap-y-1 border-t border-rule pt-5">
              <dt class="flex flex-1 basis-[240px] flex-col gap-1">
                <span class="text-[15px] font-medium text-fg">{item.what}</span>
                <span class="text-[13px] text-fg-muted">{item.when}</span>
              </dt>
              <dd class="m-0 flex-[2] basis-[420px] text-[14.5px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
                {item.body}
              </dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
      <p class="mt-6 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[14px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-muted text-pretty">
        That is the whole list. There is no analytics, no telemetry, no crash reporter and no
        first-run ping.
        <a href={DOCS.audit} rel="noopener" class="underline underline-offset-2">
          scripts/audit-listener.sh
        </a> asserts the gateway binds loopback and nothing else; the update check is the single
        documented exception to that, and it is off until you switch it on.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="m-0 font-display text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]">
        What never leaves
      </h2>
      <dl class="m-0 mt-6 flex flex-col gap-6">
        {
          STAYS.map((item) => (
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-start gap-x-10 gap-y-1 border-t border-rule pt-5">
              <dt class="flex-1 basis-[240px] text-[15px] font-medium text-fg">{item.what}</dt>
              <dd class="m-0 flex-[2] basis-[420px] text-[14.5px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
                {item.body}
              </dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="m-0 font-display text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]">
        Buying a licence
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-5 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
        This is the one place personal data exists at all, and it exists because a licence has to be
        deliverable and a refund has to be traceable back to a sale.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
        Checkout is handled by Stripe, who take the payment details — this site never sees a card
        number. What is kept afterwards is your email address, the Stripe session and payment
        identifiers, and the licence issued against them. The email is used to send you the key and
        to find the licence again if you ask for it or if a refund or dispute has to revoke it. It
        is not used for anything else, and there is no mailing list to be added to.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
        The app itself never sends your email anywhere. A key is checked offline, so an installed
        copy has no idea who bought it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="m-0 font-display text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]">This site</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
        Static files on Cloudflare, with no analytics script, no cookies and no third-party embeds.
        It loads no fonts and no images from anywhere else — the content security policy in
        <code class="font-mono text-[13.5px] text-fg/75">astro.config.mjs</code> is
        <code class="font-mono text-[13.5px] text-fg/75">'self'</code> throughout, so a request to a
        third party would not merely be absent, it would be refused by the browser. Cloudflare keeps
        its own request logs, as any host does.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="m-0 font-display text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]">Asking</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 mb-0 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
        To get a copy of what is held against your email, or to have it deleted, write to
        <a href="mailto:support@mgcrea.io" class="underline underline-offset-2">support@mgcrea.io</a>.
        Deleting the record revokes the licence it belongs to, because the record
        <em>is</em> the licence — there is nothing else it could mean.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
